<subsection><title>The INPUT tag</title>
  
  <p>The <c>input</c> tag is fairly straightforward, but you can
specify the type of input you are looking for by using the
<c>TYPE</c> attribute.  There are many possible types, and we will not
try to cover all of them; consult <url href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/input" visual="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/input">the MDN page on the input element</url>.</p>
  
  <p>Here are a few of the most common ones:</p>
<p>

<ul>

   <li><c>text</c>: allows the user to enter a word
   or short phrase. This is extremely common in web forms. It gives you a short box (there are ways to control its size) for entering information such as your name, street address, city, state, phone number, and so on.</li>
            
   <li><p><c>password</c>: allows the user to enter in a word or
   phrase, but its value isn't <em>displayed,</em> so no one can look
   over their shoulder and see it.</p></li>
            
    <li><c>email</c>:  similar to the <c>text</c> type, but the browser may check to see that the value looks like an
     email address, which helps to prevent data entry errors.</li>

     <li><c>date</c>: for entering dates.  The browser will often provide a "date picker" widget to make it easy to click on a relevant date, rather than entering it from the keyboard. If the browser doesn't provide a date picker, this type is just like a <c>text</c> input. Ideally, your form should indicate the desired format for the date, such as YYYY-MM-DD.</li>

     <li><c>time</c>: for entering times. As with dates, the browser may provide a widget make it easy to enter times. You can specify what increments you allow (every 5 minutes? every 15 minutes?) and other constraints. As with dates, you should also indicate the desired format, in case the browser falls back on a basic <c>text</c> input.</li>

    </ul></p>

    <p>The <em>purpose</em> of a form is to collect information from the user, and that information is represented as name:value <em>pairs</em>. You can think of it like a little table. For example, the pizza form might be filled out like this.</p>
      <table>
  <title>Sample Data from the Pizza Form</title>
  <tabular halign="center">
    <row header="yes" bottom="minor" >
      <cell>Name</cell>
      <cell>Value</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>customer</cell>
      <cell>Hermione Granger</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>phone</cell>
      <cell>202-555-1212</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>addr</cell>
      <cell>hgranger@hogwarts.ac.uk</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>size</cell>
      <cell>small</cell>
    </row>
       <row>
      <cell>due</cell>
      <cell>20:00</cell>
    </row>
       <row>
      <cell>instructions</cell>
      <cell>Please give to Hedwig, not Errol.</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>

</table>


    <p>As we mentioned in that list, it can be helpful to show the user what 
